research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log-3843-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log-3844-## Remarks
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log-3845-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log:3846:- **What is refuted and what is not.** The claim refuted is the transfer of [[thm-heine-borel-rn]] to arbitrary normed spaces. No general classification of normed spaces is asserted here; in particular the classical converse, that a normed space whose closed unit ball is compact must be finite-dimensional, is **not** proved anywhere here.
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log-3847-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log:3848:- **Why the linear structure does not help.** The bisection proof of [[thm-heine-borel-rn]] halves one coordinate at a time and terminates because there are finitely many coordinates. On $V$ there are infinitely many, and the standard unit vectors stay a fixed distance apart no matter how far out one looks; that is exactly the failure of total boundedness in step 5.1.
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log-3849-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log-3850-- **The relation to the published metric-space version.** [[fs-closed-and-bounded-implies-compact-in-every-metric-space]] refutes the claim for metric spaces, and its witness is $\mathbb{N}$ carrying the metric that assigns distance $1$ to distinct points — a set with no linear structure at all. The present item refutes the narrower claim about **normed** spaces, on a space that is a linear subspace of a function space and carries a genuine norm, so no reader can retreat to "the counterexample was not linear".
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-page-rn-as-a-normed-space.log-3851-
