research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5051-1.1 $V$ is a linear subspace of $\mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{N}}$, hence a real vector space: it contains $0$, and if $v_j = 0$ for $j \ge K$ and $w_j = 0$ for $j \ge K'$ then $(\lambda v + w)_j = 0$ for $j \ge \max\{K,K'\}$. [L1, L4]
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5052-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5053-1.2 The values $N_1(v)$ and $N_\infty(v)$ do not depend on the admissible $K$. If $K \le K'$ are both admissible, then splitting the sum gives $\sum_{j<K'}|v_j| = \sum_{j<K}|v_j| + \sum_{j=K}^{K'-1}|v_j|$, and the second part is a sum of zeros; and $\max\{|v_j| : j<K'\} = \max\{|v_j| : j<K\}$ because the extra entries are $0$ and the maximum over $j<K$ is $\ge |v_0| \ge 0$. [L3, L4, L5]
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5054-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5055-1.3 The hypothesis that fails is finite-dimensionality. For every $p \in \mathbb{N}$ the set $\{\, e_i : i<p \,\}$ is a subset of $V$ with $p$ elements, the map $i \mapsto e_i$ being injective because $e_i(i) = 1 \ne 0 = e_{i'}(i)$ for $i \ne i'$; and it is linearly independent, since for an injective list $l \mapsto e_{i_l}$ into it and scalars $\lambda$, evaluating $\sum_{l<q}\lambda_l e_{i_l} = 0$ at the point $i_{l_0}$ gives $\lambda_{l_0} = 0$, the list $l \mapsto \lambda_l e_{i_l}(i_{l_0})$ vanishing off the single index $l_0$. [L2, L3, L5]
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5056-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5057-2.1 $N_1$ is a norm on $V$. (N1): $N_1(v) = 0$ forces every $|v_j| = 0$ for $j<K$, hence $v = 0$; and $N_1(0) = 0$. (N2): $\lambda v$ is admissible with the same $K$ and $\sum_{j<K}|\lambda v_j| = |\lambda|\sum_{j<K}|v_j|$. (N3): with $K$ admissible for both $v$ and $w$, $\sum_{j<K}|v_j+w_j| \le \sum_{j<K}|v_j| + \sum_{j<K}|w_j|$ termwise. [step 1.2, L3, L5, L8]
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5058-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5059-2.2 $N_\infty$ is a norm on $V$. (N1): $N_\infty(v) = 0$ forces $|v_j| \le 0$ and $\ge 0$ for every $j<K$, hence $v = 0$. (N2): $\max\{|\lambda v_j|\} = |\lambda|\max\{|v_j|\}$, since $|\lambda||v_j| \le |\lambda|N_\infty(v)$ for every $j$ with equality at an index attaining the maximum. (N3): $|v_j+w_j| \le |v_j|+|w_j| \le N_\infty(v)+N_\infty(w)$ for every $j<K$, and the maximum on the left is one of those numbers. [step 1.2, L4, L5, L8]
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5060-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5061-2.3 For $m \ge 1$ the vector $u^{(m)}$ lies in $V$, and $K = m$ is admissible for it; so $N_1(u^{(m)}) = \sum_{j<m}1 = \iota(m)$ and $N_\infty(u^{(m)}) = \max\{1,\dots,1\} = 1$. [step 1.2, L3, L4, L5]
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5062-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log:5063:2.4 So $V$ has no finite basis: a basis $B$ with $q$ elements would span $V$, forcing every linearly independent subset to have at most $q$ elements, while step 1.3 produces one with $q+1$. Hence $V$ is infinite-dimensional, and [[thm-all-norms-on-rn-are-equivalent]], which is a statement about $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ for a natural $n$, does not apply to it. [step 1.3, L7]
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5064-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5065-3.1 Suppose $N_1$ and $N_\infty$ were equivalent, so that in particular $N_1(v) \le C\,N_\infty(v)$ for every $v \in V$ and some real $C > 0$. Then $\iota(m) \le C$ for every $m \ge 1$, by step 2.3. [step 2.3, L8]
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5066-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5067-4.1 That contradicts the Archimedean property, which supplies a natural $m \ge 1$ with $C < \iota(m)$. So $N_1$ and $N_\infty$ are **not** equivalent, and [A1] is false. [step 3.1, A1, L6]
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5068-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log:5069:5.1 The claim [A1] is therefore false, and the true statement in its neighbourhood is [[thm-all-norms-on-rn-are-equivalent]], whose proof spends compactness of the Euclidean unit sphere, a property step 2.4 shows $V$ has no analogue of. [step 4.1, step 2.4, A1] ∎
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5070-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5071-## Remarks
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5072-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5073-- **No classification of infinite-dimensional normed spaces is claimed here.** What is exhibited is one real vector space carrying two inequivalent norms, which is all that is needed to refute the claim.
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5074-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log:5075:- **Where the proof of [[thm-all-norms-on-rn-are-equivalent]] breaks on $V$.** That proof takes the unit sphere of $\lVert\cdot\rVert_2$, which is closed and bounded, and concludes compactness from [[thm-heine-borel-rn]] — a theorem about $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ for a natural $n$, proved by bisecting finitely many coordinates. On $V$ there is no such theorem, and indeed [[fs-heine-borel-holds-in-every-normed-space]] refutes the corresponding claim on the same space.
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5076-
research/audit/wave12-A9-dispatch/certifier-fs-all-norms-on-any-real-vector-space-are-equivalent.log-5077-- **The two norms are the honest analogues of $\lVert\cdot\rVert_1$ and $\lVert\cdot\rVert_\infty$**, and the ratio $N_1/N_\infty$ at $u^{(m)}$ is exactly $\iota(m)$, the same constant that appears in the comparison chain of [[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]] on $\mathbb{R}^{m}$. In finite dimensions that constant is a bound; on $V$ it grows without bound, and the Archimedean property is what turns that into a refutation.
